  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Group's Party Poster",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Group's Party Poster\"**\n\nThe top of a page is a poster: a surreal Y2K collage of big sky, a perspective emerald checkerboard, tall grass, oversized flowers, glossy green chess pieces and a lilac retro computer showing a pixel divide sign, with ultra-wide black caps set in the clear sky. Then the money arrives and the poster steps back. Everything someone works with sits on solid, flat panels with small corners, in a calm green-tinted neutral, and every page ends in a meadow.\n\nThe density is that of a phone tool used one-handed at a table at night: 44px targets, 17px body, tabular amounts. Personality lives in the scene, the checker details, the pawn walking along the steps and the meadow, never in the rows where somebody is reading an amount. Light and dark are the same world at different hours: a cobalt day with a white-and-emerald board, and a near-black night with a moon and a black-and-emerald board.\n\nThe world rejects the soft fintech card stack and the frosted glass it replaced. The visual language follows the user's chosen reference campaign, translated to the app's green and drawn from scratch.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "A full-bleed scene at the top of the home and shared-bill pages, breaking into the page through two rows of squares.",
      "Ultra-wide black caps for short titles and headline totals only.",
      "Solid panels, 8px corners, hairline borders, no glass.",
      "Chess as the interface's own metaphor: steps are a rank, the pawn promotes to a queen on Share.",
      "A meadow growing from the bottom of every page."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Drawn From Code Rule",
        "body": "All artwork comes from `scripts/make-art.py`, which draws the scene, meadow, share-card sky and favicons procedurally with no source images. New artwork extends that script. It must never reuse the reference campaign's images.",
        "section": "overview"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Dark On Green Rule",
        "body": "Text and icons on Brand Green are always Deep Forest On-Green, never white.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Green Text Rule",
        "body": "Brand Green is a fill, not a text colour. On white, green text is Text Green; on Soft Green, it is Strong Green.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Only Red Rule",
        "body": "In the interface, red means a negative amount or an error, and nothing else. The artwork's coral flowers and computer lights are part of the world and exempt.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Poster Type Rule",
        "body": "The display face is for short titles, totals and the headings above cards. It is never used for running text.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Money Rule",
        "body": "Amounts always use tabular figures, with the currency symbol and decimals at 62% size, so the whole number is what you read.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Date Below Rule",
        "body": "The date and details line sits under the title and total, in the scene and on the share card. It never sits above the title.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Meadow Ending Rule",
        "body": "Every page ends in the meadow, clamp(170px, 24vw, 300px) tall, growing up from the nav bar on phones and from the bottom of the screen on desktop. It sits behind the content, so the last card may overlap its grass tips. It is decorative, hidden from assistive technology and loaded lazily.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Card Rule",
        "body": "Cards have no shadow. A shadow means the element floats above the page.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Cheap To Paint Rule",
        "body": "No `backdrop-filter` or CSS blur filter in the interface; every working surface is solid, because the app runs on phones on mobile data. Soft shading baked into the rasters is part of the artwork and is fine.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do generate any new artwork with `scripts/make-art.py`, in day and night versions, and keep the provenance file beside each shipped raster.",
      "Do put poster type in the clear upper sky and keep amounts people act on in cards on solid panels.",
      "Do use dark text #04261a on Brand Green #00b368.",
      "Do end every page in the meadow and run the nav bar solid across the full width.",
      "Do place the date and details line under the title and total.",
      "Do keep touch targets at 44px or larger and set every amount in tabular figures.",
      "Do ship every new surface in both day and night.",
      "Do use `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` for all motion and let reduced-motion remove it."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't reuse or trace the reference campaign's images. Take its language, and draw everything from code.",
      "Don't add `backdrop-filter`, CSS blur or frosted surfaces to the interface.",
      "Don't put white text on Brand Green, or use Brand Green itself as text.",
      "Don't use red in the interface for anything but negative amounts and errors.",
      "Don't add a shadow to a card that is not floating.",
      "Don't float the navigation as a pill or make it translucent.",
      "Don't set running text in the poster face or place a small line above a bill title.",
      "Don't let the scene follow people into the working area: below the header, surfaces stay calm and solid."
    ]
  }
}